# The pgpushy release this action installs, and the SHA-256 of each of the
# four binaries that release publishes.
#
# One pinned version, and no input to override it: this action's end-to-end
# tests run exactly one pgpushy, so any other version would be a compatibility
# claim nothing here checks. A consumer pins pgpushy by pinning the action.
#
# The hashes ship with the action rather than being read from the release at
# install time. A SHA256SUMS fetched from the same origin as the binary catches
# a corrupted download and nothing else; a table reviewed alongside the version
# bump that introduces it catches a release that was replaced. That is the
# argument pgpushy makes for pgschema (pgpushy spec §8.5), pointed one level up.
#
# sha256sum format with bare filenames, so `sha256sum -c pgpushy.pin` verifies a
# directory holding all four assets — which is what CI does on every run. The
# version is the one in these asset names; the README's "Which pgpushy" section
# also states it in prose, because a reader deciding whether to adopt this
# action should not have to open a checksum file to find out what it runs.
# scripts/pin-check.sh requires all four rows and that sentence to agree.
#
# Rewritten by `just bump-pgpushy <X.Y.Z>`, which refuses unless the hashes it
# computes from the downloaded assets, the release's own SHA256SUMS, and
# GitHub's per-asset digests all agree.
8d7c8f40c6971b0eb536a4ad5e1c968df30141c571f9471af36cdd49ebdd20da  pgpushy-0.3.2-linux-amd64
